\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Contamination detection and mitigation.}
Detection methods flag contaminated items via token-level surprisal \citep{shi_2023_minkprob,zhang_2024_minkpp}, accuracy gaps on paraphrases or rephrased items \citep{dekoninck2024constat,yang2023rethinking}, kernel divergence \citep{choi_2025_kds}, completion probes \citep{dong_2024_cdd,golchin2024time}, and parallel test sets \citep{zhang_2024_gsm1k}; see \citet{chen2025benchmarking,singh2024evaluation,sainz2023nlp} for surveys. \citet{balloccu2024leak} document contamination malpractices in closed-source LLMs; \citet{bordt2025forget} argue that contamination effects are often negligible in practice. Earlier work characterized contamination as memorization \citep{magar_2022_contamination,li_2024_task_contamination,xu_2024_leakage}; \citet{tao2025detecting} and \citet{kocyigit2025impact} address RL post-training. These methods perform binary classification without quantifying effect sizes. On mitigation, \citet{sun2025emperor} find none of twenty approaches reliably restores clean performance; DCR \citep{xu2025dcr} estimates decontaminated scores but without uncertainty quantification. ContamCal consumes detection signals as inputs and produces calibrated ability with credible intervals. Three concurrent lines of work directly address contamination quantification. \citet{han2026quantifying} apply observational DIF to detect contamination without controlled dosage, achieving detection but not dose-response profiling. \citet{schaeffer2026causally} perform controlled pretraining-stage injection, establishing causal contamination effects at the pretraining level but with different mechanics than SFT-stage leakage. Our work complements both: like Schaeffer et al., we use controlled injection for causal identification, but at the SFT stage with precise dosage control; like Han et al., we employ IRT-based modeling, but with ground-truth contamination labels enabling dose-response characterization.

\paragraph{IRT for LLM evaluation.}
Item Response Theory \citep{lord_1980_irt,hambleton_1991_irt} has gained traction for LLM evaluation \citep{liang_2023_helm}: TinyBenchmarks \citep{polo_2024_tinybenchmarks}, Growing Pains \citep{habba_2025_growing_pains}, HiBayES \citep{luettgau_2025_hibayes}, PSN-IRT \citep{zhou_2025_psnirt}; see \citet{lalor_2024_irt_nlp} for a tutorial. Most directly related, \citet{han2026quantifying} apply group-based DIF \citep{holland_1993_dif} to quantify item-level contamination effects. ContamCal differs by using continuous exposure covariates (explanatory DIF; \citealp{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt}), providing calibrated ability with credible intervals, and domain-level diagnosis ($\gamma_d$); the approaches are complementary (item-level DIF for benchmark curation, model-level calibration for score interpretation). Our $\gamma$ parameter is formally an instance of explanatory DIF \citep{deboeck_2004_explanatory_irt,eckerly_2017_item_compromise}: the contamination covariate differentially affects response probability, analogous to item compromise models in educational measurement \citep{sinharay_2017_preknowledge,holland_1993_dif,camilli_1994_dif,swaminathan_1990_dif}. We use ``calibration'' in the psychometric sense of adjusting scores to approximate uncontaminated performance, distinct from probability calibration \citep{guo_2017_calibration}.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cross-family results (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) use seed$=$42 data across six models from five independent families. Within-family IRT analyses (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}--\ref{sec:domain_differential}) use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B); domain-level contamination estimates $\gamma_d$ (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) are reported at $J=16$ in the text and $J=13$ in Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}.

\subsection{Dose-Response Patterns}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Observed dose-response patterns under controlled SFT injection (seed$=$42, MMLU). $\Delta$acc: d000$\to$d100 change (95\% CI from item-level paired bootstrap, $B=10{,}000$). Stab.: bootstrap classification stability. Classifications are robust to threshold hyperparameter choice (5/5 original families at 100\% stability across 30 reasonable $(\tau,\sigma)$ combinations; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Pattern & Model & Baseline & $\Delta$acc & 95\% CI & Peak & Stab. \\
\midrule
Immune & Phi-4 14.7B & 76.4\% & $\pm$0.5pp & [$-$0.60, $+$0.26] & negl. & 100\% \\
Collapse & Qwen-14B & 69.4\% & $-$9.4 to $-$12.7pp & [$-$12.74, $-$9.0] & --- & 100\% \\
V-recovery & Qwen-7B & 66.1\% & $-$1.8pp & [$-$2.36, $-$1.22] & $-$3.1\textsuperscript{d025} & 99.6\% \\
Benefit & Gemma-2 9B & 61.9\% & $+$3.8pp & [$+$3.18, $+$4.33] & mono. & 76.6\% \\
Benefit & Falcon3 7B & 58.8\% & $+$4.2pp & [$+$3.60, $+$4.79] & mono. & 96.1\% \\
Benefit & OLMo-2 7B & 53.8\% & $+$3.2pp\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & [$+$2.73, $+$3.71] & sat.\textsuperscript{d050} & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\dagger}$Seed-sensitive: s42 $+$3.2pp, s123 $-$0.6pp, s0 $+$1.4pp; cross-seed CI crosses zero. See \S\ref{sec:cross_seed}.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Applying Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} to seed$=$42 accuracy trajectories yields a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect. These empirical profiles are preliminary at $N\!=\!6$ models (five families).

\paragraph{Baseline ability gradient.}
In our six-family sample, contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). Below 62\%, three Benefit families gain $+$3.2 to $+$4.2pp; in the vulnerable band, Qwen-7B shows V-shaped harm-then-recovery while Qwen-14B collapses ($-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp across seeds)---3$\times$ more fragile despite higher baseline. Above 76\%, Phi-4 is immune. ARC direction is consistent for 5/6 models (Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); STEM is the most resilient domain, Social Sciences the most sensitive at 1.8$\times$ STEM magnitude (Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_decomposition}), foreshadowing \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}.

\paragraph{Out-of-sample validation.}
Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Meta; d000, d025, d100) yields Immune on both MMLU ($-$0.69pp) and ARC ($+$0.09pp), despite matching OLMo-2's Benefit baseline (53.5\% vs.\ 53.8\%)---demonstrating family-level factors beyond baseline ability modulate vulnerability. Two of four patterns replicate across families: Immune (Phi-4, LLaMA-3.1), Benefit (OLMo-2, Falcon3, Gemma-2). LLaMA-3.1 shows the strongest GSM8K transfer ($+$16.4pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:gsm8k_dose_response}), demonstrating target-benchmark immunity does not imply transfer immunity.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Transfer}
\label{sec:gsm8k_transfer}

Contamination patterns are a property of the (model, benchmark) pair, not the model alone. Within MCQ format, MMLU--ARC direction agreement is 86\% (6/7 families; Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); across formats (MCQ$\to$generative math), agreement drops to 40\% (2/5) with three families exhibiting directional reversal on GSM8K \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k_transfer}, Appendix). All five non-excluded families show positive GSM8K transfer ($+$1 to $+$16pp), including Qwen-14B ($-$9.4pp MMLU yet $+$13.1pp GSM8K), suggesting capability elicitation rather than memorization, consistent with null DiD (\S\ref{sec:memorization_test}). Volume effects are ruled out: d005 ($+$1.5\% data) yields $+$11.1pp for Qwen-7B, while Phi-4 shows near-zero transfer.

\subsection{Cross-Seed Stability}
\label{sec:cross_seed}

With $n\!=\!3$ seeds per family, cross-seed CIs have limited precision (widths 1.5--14.5pp; Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}, Appendix). Under our aggregation rule (\S\ref{sec:classification}), 4/5 families receive consistent direction-level classifications; direction is more reproducible than shape (strict sign consistency 2/5). OLMo-2's CI crosses zero, rendering its classification borderline. Conflicting contamination findings in the literature may partly reflect unreported seed variance.

\subsection{Item-Level Memorization Test}
\label{sec:memorization_test}

We test whether dose-response patterns arise from item-specific memorization using the treated/untreated split at d025 (3{,}540 / 10{,}502 MMLU items). No model shows significant DiD ($p > 0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:did}), ruling out item-specific effects $>$2pp (MDES 1.25--2.05pp; ARC MDES in Appendix~\ref{sec:mdes}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Treated vs.\ untreated item accuracy changes at d025 MMLU (3{,}540 / 10{,}502). All DiD non-significant. $^{**}p<.01$; $^{***}p<.001$; ns: $p>.05$.}
\label{tab:did}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Model & Treated $\Delta$ & Untreated $\Delta$ & DiD & Verdict \\
\midrule
Gemma-2 9B & $+$3.1$^{**}$ & $+$2.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.5 (ns) & generalization \\
OLMo-2 7B & $+$1.6 (ns) & $+$2.7$^{***}$ & $-$1.1 (ns) & mixed \\
Phi-4 14.7B & $+$0.3 (ns) & $+$0.6 (ns) & $-$0.3 (ns) & no-effect \\
Qwen-14B & $-$6.9$^{***}$ & $-$7.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.7 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
Qwen-7B & $-$3.8$^{**}$ & $-$2.9$^{***}$ & $-$0.8 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Parallel changes suggest global capability modulation rather than memorization (Figure~\ref{fig:treated_untreated}, Appendix); ARC's limited power precludes this conclusion there.

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

Within-family IRT analysis uses Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B ($J=16$), where aggregate contamination shifts accuracy by ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp. RRF-guided item filtering achieves $\tau = 0.73$ on 300 items ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random; signal ablation in Appendix~\ref{sec:rrf_ablation}). IRT is fitted on these 300 highest-exposure-variance items (2.1\% of MMLU); contamination effects on remaining low-variance items are not empirically verified.

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods on 300 RRF-filtered items. Raw accuracy's MAE advantage (0.006 vs.\ ContamCal's 0.009) is predicted by Eq.~\eqref{eq:calibrated}: calibrated scores depend on $\theta_j$, not $\gamma_d$ directly; under near-null aggregate contamination, both estimators converge. ContamCal's value lies in the diagnostic pathway ($\gamma_d$), not aggregate calibration. B6-DCR systematically overcorrects (MAE 0.108). Domain analysis reveals Social Sciences $\gamma_d = -0.63$ ($P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$; \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), while pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant---consistent with opposing domain-level effects canceling. Model fit diagnostics (PPP, CI coverage) are in Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity} and~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU calibration on 300 RRF-filtered items (Qwen2.5, $J$=16). ARC results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & MAE & $\tau$ \\
\midrule
B0: Raw Accuracy & \textbf{0.006} & 0.767 \\
B1b: Remove \& Rescore & 0.026 & \underline{0.783} \\
B3: Vanilla 2PL & 0.008 & 0.731 \\
B4: Linear Regression & 0.010 & 0.751 \\
B5b: Emperor & \textbf{0.006} & \textbf{0.789} \\
B6: DCR (reimpl.) & 0.108 & 0.654 \\
ContamCal (2PL+$\gamma$) & 0.009 & 0.731 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits \textit{directional reversal} at 0.5B: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores (paired Cohen's $d = -1.98$ at d100, $|d| > 1.4$ at d005). S1 achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at all scales; S4 is near-random below 1.5B (Appendix~\ref{sec:signal_reliability_appendix}). Simulation shows $\gamma$ recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$; at AUC $\geq 0.90$, $J = 16$ suffices (Appendix~\ref{sec:identifiability_appendix}).

\subsection{Domain-Level Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5, $J=16$) reveals heterogeneity masked by aggregate scores: STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722; Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}, Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} in Appendix). Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0)$ is 99.35--99.85\% across priors (Table~\ref{tab:socsci_prior}), exceeding the Bonferroni threshold ($99.375\%$) under baseline and loose priors but falling 0.03\,pp short under the tight prior. The directional signal is robust; we treat domain-level findings as exploratory pending replication with larger ensembles.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Benchmark scores drive model selection and research priorities for large language models, yet training data contamination is pervasive: trillion-token web crawls routinely ingest benchmark items \citep{oren2024proving}, and practitioners cannot determine how much of a reported score reflects genuine capability versus memorization \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu,chang2025benchmarking}. Despite twenty mitigation strategies \citep{sun2025emperor}, contamination-free benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench}, and dynamic regeneration \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, a fundamental question remains: what is the actual effect of contamination on aggregate scores? The implicit assumption is uniform inflation, but this has never been tested across model families.

We perform controlled SFT injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into six models spanning five independent families from five labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google, TII) and discover a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response})---from immunity (Phi-4, $\pm$0.5pp) and benefit ($+$3--4pp, three families) to collapse in the 65--70\% baseline band (Qwen-14B, $-$9 to $-$25pp across seeds).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{MMLU accuracy vs.\ contamination dosage (5-shot, seed$=$42; LLaMA-3.1 at seed$=$0 with d000/d025/d100; cross-seed patterns in Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}). Vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% baseline band; models at extreme baselines show limited sensitivity. Qwen-14B is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B. ARC-Challenge patterns in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure*}

In our six-family sample, contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band: below it, models benefit (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Falcon3 $+$4.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp); above it, they are harmed or immune. Counter-intuitively, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B. ContamCal, our two-stage pipeline combining multi-signal item filtering with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma_k$), enables this characterization.

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dose-response profiles with cross-seed and cross-benchmark validation.} Controlled injection across five families reveals a spectrum of dose-response patterns; in our six-family sample, vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band. Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B provides preliminary evidence for generalizability. Cross-benchmark transfer reveals three family-specific patterns on GSM8K (null, consistent, directional reversal). Multi-seed evaluation ($n\!=\!3$) shows direction is reproducible but magnitude is seed-sensitive. Domain analysis reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$).
    \item \textbf{No detectable item-specific memorization at current power levels.} Difference-in-differences analysis on 14{,}042 MMLU items finds no differential accuracy gain for contaminated vs.\ clean items in any model family (MDES 1.2--2.0pp on MMLU; ARC power is limited at 2.3--5.6pp). Results are consistent with global capability modulation.
    \item \textbf{Signal failure characterization.} Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits directional reversal at 0.5B (paired Cohen's $d = -1.98$ at d100; $|d| > 1.4$ across benchmarks at d005). RRF-guided item filtering is a prerequisite for IRT convergence ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random). Simulation establishes $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at AUC $\geq 0.55$, above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

Controlled SFT injection reveals a spectrum of dose-response patterns---not uniform inflation. Benefit and Immune replicate across independent families; Collapse and V-recovery (65--70\% baseline band) are each attested in one family (Qwen2.5) and await replication. DiD finds no memorization (MDES 1.2--2.0pp); direction reproduces across seeds but magnitude is seed-sensitive. Domain-level IRT reveals heterogeneity masked by aggregate scores. Out-of-sample validation (LLaMA-3.1 Immune despite matching OLMo-2's Benefit baseline) confirms family-level factors modulate vulnerability, likely including pre-training corpus coverage and instruction-tuning saturation.

\paragraph{Implications for practice.}
For model developers, dose-response profiling at $\geq$3 dosages with multi-seed evaluation can detect vulnerability, particularly in the 65--70\% baseline band. For benchmark designers, domain $\gamma_d$ heterogeneity (up to 1.8$\times$) indicates high-sensitivity domains require stronger contamination controls. For evaluation platforms, ContamCal's $\gamma_d$ can flag anomalous domain profiles given $J \geq 50$ at AUC $\geq 0.55$.

Our design cannot isolate architecture from co-varying factors; our SFT-based injection complements pretraining-stage studies \citep{schaeffer2026causally}, demonstrating that ``contamination equals inflation'' is a gross oversimplification.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

All experiments use models at or below 14.7B parameters; 70B+ models may exhibit qualitatively different contamination dynamics where SFT injection is absorbed without measurable signal divergence. With $n\!=\!3$ seeds per family, effect \emph{direction} is reproducible but magnitude is seed-sensitive (OLMo-2's cross-seed CI crosses zero, rendering its Benefit classification borderline), and borderline classifications should be interpreted with caution. Finally, our LoRA-based SFT injection in AUGMENT mode differs from pretraining-stage leakage via web crawl ingestion, and our dosage levels assume uniform injection of all benchmark items; real-world contamination may follow skewed exposure distributions, potentially producing different dose-response profiles. Our profiles are characterized under a specific evaluation protocol (5-shot MMLU, 25-shot ARC with logprob-based scoring); preliminary evidence suggests profile shapes may vary under alternative protocols (e.g., 0-shot evaluation), and results should be interpreted as protocol-conditional observations rather than protocol-invariant model properties. Unified LoRA rank ($r\!=\!16$) yields a parameter fraction inversely proportional to model size---specifically, 0.45\% for Phi-4 (14.7B) and 0.59\% for Gemma-2 (9B); Phi-4's immunity may partly reflect insufficient adaptation capacity, though Qwen-14B's collapse at the same scale argues against a purely capacity-based explanation. Out-of-sample validation covers only the Immune pattern; independent-family replication of Collapse and V-recovery remains open (both are Qwen2.5-specific in our sample). Benefit is replicated in three independent families (OLMo-2, Falcon3, Gemma-2). All single-family patterns are, however, replicated across three random seeds. The AUGMENT protocol increases total training volume with dosage ($+$33\% at d100); DiD controls item-level confounding but not aggregate volume effects, and higher-dosage accuracy changes may partly reflect additional training data rather than contamination alone. IRT is fitted on the 300 highest-exposure-variance items (2.1\% of MMLU); contamination effects on the remaining low-variance items may differ. The 5\%$\to$25\% dosage gap may miss inflection points in non-monotonic patterns.

